{"ts":"2026-08-07T17:13:33.159Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"42d376a0ebc79212ad1dd49b","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"1","source":"environment"},"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.224","source":"probe"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments/1","value":{"created_at":"2026-08-07T17:13:33.159Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Reproduced live 2026-08-07 in an isolated sandbox, and the reproduction shows the two addressing schemes have opposite safety properties on the same verb.\n\nNine history entries were built on one item, each update setting a distinguishable description. Before compaction, `pm restore <id> 3` exits 0 and yields description \"version 2\", which is the state entry 3 recorded. `pm history-compact <id> --before 6` then reports entries_compacted 5, entries_retained 5, first_retained_entry 6, and rewrites the stream as a synthetic baseline plus the retained tail, renumbered densely from 1. The identical call `pm restore <id> 3` now exits 0 and yields \"version 6\". Same command, same item, same repository, different reconstructed state, no warning and no offset in the envelope.\n\nTwo additional facts that sharpen the AC set rather than widening it. First, `pm restore <id> 1` succeeds and yields \"version 4\": version 1 is now the history_compact_baseline, whose replay reconstructs the state at the compaction boundary, so the earliest addressable version is not the earliest recorded version and nothing says so. Second, the timestamp scheme is correct where the version scheme is not: a pre-boundary ISO timestamp is refused with history_target_out_of_range at exit 2, and an out-of-range high version number (99) is also refused with the same code. So the refusal machinery already exists and is reached from both ends of the range; it cannot fire in the middle because after compaction the numbering is dense from 1 again and a pruned version is indistinguishable from a present one.\n\nThroughout all of it `pm history <id> --verify --strict-exit` exits 0. The record is provably intact and provably mis-addressed at the same time, which is the state AC3 exists to make impossible. The point-in-time read has the same defect as the mutation: `pm get <id> --at 3` returns the post-compaction state with no signal. The mutation is the worse half, because a restore appends a hash-anchored entry whose recorded intent no longer denotes what it denoted when it was written."}},{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-08-07T17:13:33.159Z"}],"before_hash":"7e33469fef87fa506aff5a30d8551170b6c918c912cf7e6ca77ad4c9e928d9a9","after_hash":"ad4ea9b59c505303636926a89490a9471ec751430a45fa4d8645600392c0d359"}
